    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d233">

                <pb facs="0327" n="247" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg247"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.003/2248</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cotton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-05-06">May 6, 1930</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">The Secretary</hi>: The attached memorandum is all
                    correct. I took this up with the President some time ago and also with the
                    Chairman of the Conference Committee. I have letters<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d233-f1"/> from both Smoot and Hawley<anchor n="60" xml:id="d233-f2"
                    /> saying it is impossible to make the changes. The Department has done all it
                    can to this end and has a clear record. There is no excuse for these
                    countervailing duties, but I do not recommend further action.</p>
                <closer><signed>J. P. C[<hi rendition="#small-caps">otton</hi>]</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d233-a1.xml" xpointer="d233-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d233-f1" n="59" xml:id="d233-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d233-f2" n="60" xml:id="d233-f2-content">
                <p>Representative Willis C. Hawley, Chairman of the House Ways and Means
                    Committee.</p>
            </note>
